<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!-- THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT. A comment placed ABOVE
         <CustomField> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading "unable to find
         matching parent xml file" error (same gotcha documented on the Offering__c precedent,
         objects/Offering__c/fields/Is_Owner_Queue__c.field-meta.xml).

         RECREATED 2026-09-01. This field was deleted earlier in this session as part of a
         feature rollback that has now been partially reversed - restoring it exactly as it was
         originally built (same formula, not placed on any layout, same read-only FLS grants on
         the same three permission sets).

         WHY THIS FIELD EXISTS: Data Cloud's Contact data stream needs to filter/segment on the
         Contact's record type, but a Data Lake Object (DLO) cannot traverse the relationship
         expression RecordType.DeveloperName directly - Data Cloud only maps flat fields that
         live on the source object itself, not fields reached through a lookup/relationship
         traversal. This formula flattens RecordType.DeveloperName onto Contact as plain text so
         it is directly mappable into the DLO and usable as a Data Cloud filter/segmentation
         criterion.

         DERIVED, NEVER WRITTEN. This is a FORMULA field, not a stored value - nothing sets it
         directly and it cannot drift out of sync with the Contact's actual RecordTypeId.

         NOT ON ANY LAYOUT, DELIBERATELY. This field exists purely for the Data Cloud data
         stream to consume, not for a human to read on the record page - a Metadata-API-deployed
         field is not auto-added to any layout, so simply omitting it here is sufficient.

         FLS IS LOAD-BEARING. A field deployed through the Metadata API arrives with NO field
         permissions for ANY profile, System Administrator included - the read grants ship in
         the same change (DPEG_IR_Default_Access, DPEG_IR_Principle_Read, DPEG_Admin_Access),
         and formula fields are read-only by construction, so no `editable` grant is meaningful
         here. DPEG_Apex_Access never carried this grant (it only ever holds classAccesses) and
         the DPEG-IR-SF-Minimum Access profile never carried it either - neither is touched by
         this restore. -->
    <fullName>Record_Type_Name__c</fullName>
    <description>Exposes the Contact's record type developer name as plain text so Data Cloud can filter/segment the Contact data stream on it - Data Cloud cannot traverse RecordType.DeveloperName directly on a DLO. Derived, never written directly. Not placed on any layout.</description>
    <externalId>false</externalId>
    <formula><![CDATA[RecordType.DeveloperName]]></formula>
    <formulaTreatBlanksAs>BlankAsBlank</formulaTreatBlanksAs>
    <inlineHelpText>System field for Data Cloud. Mirrors RecordType.DeveloperName as plain text.</inlineHelpText>
    <label>Record Type Name</label>
    <required>false</required>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Text</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
